story-label = One request, from the kitchen to Léa’s phone
story-ask-title = You say it, once.
story-ask-text = Aloud in the kitchen, or in a message. No app to open, no rule to write: one sentence is enough.
story-ask-room = Kitchen
story-ask-said = “Tell me when the school writes back.”
story-ask-answer = Sure, I’ll watch your mail and let you know.
story-watch-title = It keeps an eye on it.
story-watch-text = The conversation ends, the watch goes on. Each mail is read once, and only the one that matters is kept.
story-watch-name = Watch for Léa
story-watch-looks = The school’s answer about the trip
story-watch-live = active
story-watch-letter = This week’s newsletter
story-watch-bill = Your electricity bill
story-watch-school = School: Friday’s trip is on
story-watch-dropped = set aside
story-watch-kept = for Léa
story-found-title = It finds Léa, wherever she is.
story-found-text = Home Assistant knows she is out. So the message goes to her phone, not to an empty kitchen.
story-found-phone = Léa’s phone, with Nestor’s message
story-found-date = Wednesday, November 18
story-found-now = now
story-found-message = The school wrote back: Friday’s trip is on. Pack a picnic.
story-reminder-title = At home, it speaks in the right room.
story-reminder-text = A reminder, an answer, a message for someone else: it says it where the person is, and never cuts into a conversation.
story-reminder-room = Grandma’s room
story-reminder-said = “It’s eight o’clock, Grandma: your pills.”
story-reminder-why = Grandma is in her room
story-why-title = And you always know why.
story-why-text = Every word it says is in the household’s journal, with its reason. You can read it again in the admin, at any time.
story-why-journal = Journal
story-why-asked = Léa wants to know when the school writes back
story-why-watch = Watch created for Léa
